Device Template Definition (Gauge & Grab)

The device_template.json file defines a single, shared data contract used by all three food locations in the Gauge & Grab project:

Pool Street Café

Marshgate Café

Marshgate Canteen

Rather than creating separate schemas per location, the project uses one unified device template, ensuring that:

all MQTT messages follow the same structure,

physical and digital devices can subscribe without custom parsing,

the system remains scalable and easy to extend.

Each location is distinguished at runtime by:

its MQTT topic, and

the location field inside the payload.

Exact device_template.json Used in the Project
{
  "device_type": "GaugeAndGrab",
  "root_topic": "student/CASA0019/gauge&grab",
  "description": "Unified device template for all Gauge & Grab food locations. This template defines the MQTT payload structure used by the physical gauge, digital gauge, and digital twin dashboards.",
  "payload_structure": {
    "location": "string",
    "queue_time": "integer",
    "queue_band": "string",
    "daily_special": "string",
    "price": "float",
    "weekday": "string",
    "timestamp": "ISO-8601"
  }
}

How This Template Was Used for All Three Locations
1. Topic Differentiation

Each location publishes to a location-specific MQTT topic under the same root:

student/CASA0019/gauge&grab/pool_street/state
student/CASA0019/gauge&grab/marshgate_cafe/state
student/CASA0019/gauge&grab/marshgate_canteen/state


The same device template applies to all three topics.

2. Runtime Data Injection (Python)

At runtime, the Python data generator populates the template fields with live values, depending on the location being processed.

Example (Pool Street Café):

{
  "location": "pool_street",
  "queue_time": 4,
  "queue_band": "0-5",
  "daily_special": "Chicken Wrap",
  "price": 4.95,
  "weekday": "Tuesday",
  "timestamp": "2026-01-06T12:30:00"
}


Example (Marshgate Canteen):

{
  "location": "marshgate_canteen",
  "queue_time": 12,
  "queue_band": "10+",
  "daily_special": "Curry Bowl",
  "price": 6.20,
  "weekday": "Tuesday",
  "timestamp": "2026-01-06T12:30:00"
}

3. Why a Single Template Was Chosen

Using one shared template:

guarantees data consistency across devices,

simplifies Arduino and Unity subscription logic,

supports physical–digital synchronisation,

aligns with IoT best practices and DaBo design principles.

This approach allows any subscribing device to rely on a stable payload schema, regardless of which location is selected.

Relationship to Live Data Generation

During execution:

Python generates dummy queue and menu values per location.

Values are mapped to the fields defined in payload_structure.

The payload is serialised as JSON.

The message is published via MQTT using the root_topic.

Physical and digital devices receive and interpret the data using the same schema.

This ensures that live MQTT data remains predictable, readable, and reusable across the entire system.
